{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310754,"nonce":"1RJHrXIZJnhLSA98unWQBMZMYdY=","title":"Three wasm defects made Tensor f32 data silently load as zeros/garbage where native was correct. ALL FIXED dea69ddf: (1) GEP/PtrAdd ...","message":"Found extending the nue pure-Haxe kernel work: on wasm, `Tensor.fromArray(arr.push(...))` + `getFlat`/`Ptr` reads returned 0/garbage while native was right. THREE independent bugs, all fixed in commit dea69ddf (regression-free: native haxe suite 137 pass, test_simd_e2e 17/17, test_tensor_e2e 20/20, TensorDemo/SimdDemo/concurrency coherent on wasm).\n\n1. **GEP / PtrAdd pointee-size scaling (compiler/src/codegen/wasm_backend.rs).** The two inline `elem_size` matches (GetElementPtr ~2920, PtrAdd ~2948) scaled `U8|I8=\u003e1, _=\u003e8` — i.e. EVERYTHING non-byte by 8. So an f32 tensor buffer (`Ptr\u003cF32\u003e`) strode 8 bytes per index and read every OTHER lane as garbage; getFlat looked right only because it's an FFI call, but Haxe-side f32 indexing/SIMD-load offsets were wrong. FIX: new `gep_pointee_size(ty)` helper — `f32/i32/u32=\u003e4, i16/u16=\u003e2, i8/u8=\u003e1, else 8` (I64/F64/Ptr/Void/struct keep the 8-byte slot stride the rest of the backend assumes, e.g. Ptr\u003cI64\u003e array headers at 0/8/16/24). Mirrors native build_ptr_add's pointee-size scaling. This was the user's lead (\"look into how wasm_encode generates GEP\").\n\n2. **Array\u003cFloat\u003e.push truncated to 0 on wasm (compiler/src/ir/hir_to_mir.rs).** The generic `array_push` MIR wrapper declares value:I64, but **IrType::I64 lowers to a wasm i32** (rayzor's wasm is 32-bit; wasm_backend val-type `_ =\u003e I32`). A Float pushed as the f64 bit-pattern bitcast-to-i64 loses its high 32 bits → reads back 0. Array LITERALS `[1.5,2.5]` already worked (lower_array_literal dispatches F64→push_f64 at ~33108). FIX: intercept `arr.push(x)` in the **Variable-callee** method path (vname==\"push\", is_method, args.len()==2 where args[0]=receiver, args[1]=value — the desugared shape ALL receiver shapes use: variable, field `b.arr.push`, call-result `getArr().push`; the Field-callee `method_name==\"push\"` path NEVER fires for push, confirmed). When the receiver's element type is F64, route to `haxe_array_push_f64` (value stays F64 → wasm f64, 8 bytes). Bit-identical on native. GOTCHA that cost time: `rayzor` BINARY is the ROOT Cargo.toml package, NOT `-p compiler` — `cargo build -p compiler` compiles the lib but does NOT relink the binary, so edits silently don't take. Build with `cargo build --release --features llvm-backend -p rayzor --bin rayzor`.\n\n3. **Tensor.rand zero-fill placeholder (runtime-wasm/src/tensor.rs).** In-guest `rayzor_tensor_rand` just called `tensor_zeros` → wasm benches/tests silently ran on all-zeros. FIX: deterministic LCG fill (seed 12345, mul 6364136223846793005, add 1442695040888963407, `(seed\u003e\u003e33) as f32 / (1\u003c\u003c31)`). NOT cross-RNG parity with native (native uses a real RNG) — parity tests must use deterministic inputs — but stops the silent all-zeros.\n\nDebugging method that worked: `RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR=1` showed `fp.push(1.5)` emitting `bitcast f64-\u003ei64; call @array_push` (fn290081, the I64 wrapper) vs the literal path's `call @haxe_array_push_f64` (fn6). Related: [[bugs_simd4f_broken_two_part]] (same session — .get(lane) fix gives the f32 scalar-read).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_wasm_tensor_data_loading` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}